\title{From Rust to Lean}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The step that connects a verified protocol to a prover people actually run. Extracting [Plonky3](dhsorens-003I) into Lean and proving the extraction agrees with the specification is the current problem.
}

\p{
    To work through:
    \ul{
        \li{hax and its Lean backend: what Rust it accepts, and what it does with the parts it does not.}
        \li{The functional-equivalence obligation: extracted code against [CompPoly](dhsorens-0041)-level specifications.}
        \li{What is assumed rather than proved — unsafe blocks, arithmetic representations, parallelism.}
        \li{Keeping an extraction current against an upstream repository that moves weekly.}
        \li{Where proof automation helps, and what the AI-prover pipeline experience report actually reports.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [A Rust-to-Lean Verification Pipeline with AI Provers](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.30106), [Verified-zkEVM overview](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/Overview).
}
